Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

INT. MEETING ROOM - FRIDAY - DAY

Pop music rises each time the warehouse door below opens. Zoe tests Westmere, not a whole institution-shaped villain.

A sample ends RETURN HOME, then three months of support and closure. Another permanent placement has an outside solicitor and formal panel. No BRG, sponsor or controlled document route.

Three headings: ORDINARY / SUSPICIOUS / UNKNOWN.

ZOE
Unknown isn't suspicious written politely. If we release everything, a lawful family could read its route as proof it was stolen.

ELIAS
And the real contradiction gets buried.

She writes NO WHOLE-DRIVE DUMP.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - INTERCUT

A lawful reunification file. Vale remembers a father crying before his child reaches the door. Reyes carries the broken suitcase, then looks away from the embrace.

Voss calls a retired transport coordinator through an ordinary route. She remembers messy, real work: councils or courts decided; Westmere arranged placement and transport. It could not issue removal powers. She knew no BRG. An unknown caller recently asked about retained files and that term.

They forward a narrow account without her identity or private child detail.

INT. MEETING ROOM - LATER

Public descriptions and outside legal information set a limit around Westmere's power. Zoe draws the lawful work in black. Red lines cut through selected permissions, documents and sponsor routes; they don't cover the page.

ZOE
Not another whole organisation. Something using the real one.

ELIAS
Then some workers may become witnesses.

ZOE
Some will defend what they know was good. We shouldn't lie about that to prove what wasn't.

She closes the sample, leaves the innocent identities untouched.

At night she writes: FIND SOMEONE OUTSIDE THE LOOP. SHOW WHAT CARRIES THEIR AUTHORITY.

ZOE
Is this yours?

The question is smaller than the archive. It can be answered.
